Time 58 after 3.1s, not 57. Probably `dt` still low? 3.1s -> elapsed ~1.9? Hmm speed ~? With cap .1, maybe rAF runs at 2fps? No. Actually startGame calls reset and sets `lastFrame=performance.now()`; then immediately eval's promise, 3.1s. Frame times should sum 3.1 unless tab throttled. It shows 58 means elapsed >1? displayedTime ceil(60-elapsed)=58 => elapsed in (1,2]. So game runs at ~half speed still. Could be because rendering via SwiftShader is slow and frame interval >100ms; dt capped 0.1 loses time. FPS ~20? 3.1s / elapsed 1.6 => 12 fps, dt capped .1, lose 40%. Hmm. In a real browser 60fps fine. But on low-end mobile maybe 30fps -> dt .033 <.1. Fine. The Playwright environment is slow because canvas background gradients with many draws? It may run at 20 fps. We could remove cap entirely but use max .25 to handle. Set 0.25 to be robust at 4 fps. Collision tunneling at speed 470*.25=117px could skip obstacles (player body 28 + obstacle 43=71), causing missed hits. But rare. Could implement collision with swept movement? overkill. Use .12 maybe; still at 12fps .083 <.12 so actual. Wait current cap .1, and if fps ~12 (dt .083), should not cap. Then elapsed should ~3.1. But got 1.6. Maybe rAF ~6fps (dt .16 cap .1) => time 2s. So 6fps. Could raise cap to .25 to allow 4fps, and at 6fps dt .16 real. Collision step 470*.16=75px, slightly above 71. Could tunnel occasionally. But on such slow device, gameplay is janky anyway. We could do collision substeps: update physics in substeps of max .05. That's robust. But complexity.

Alternative don't cap dt for elapsed/time but cap movement updates? We can use `dt = min(.1,...)` for physics but still use real dt for elapsed? Then game time runs real while game updates slow. Not ideal.

Better implement fixed timestep accumulator? Complex.

Maybe use dt cap 0.2. Collision tunneling risk at 75px but obstacles spawn at intervals and widths; player width 28 body, so overlap window 71px. At speed 470 and dt .2 => 94px, can skip. Could do 2 substeps when dt>.08: run update logic in chunks. Structure update currently handles all. We could call update with dt split recursively:
```
function update(dt) {
  if (state==='playing' && dt > 0.05) { const steps=Math.ceil(dt/0.05); for... update(dt/steps); return; }
  ... existing
}
```
But update also calls updateEffects, which would then run N times and update scaled dt, fine. Recursion guard: substeps <= .05, no further split. Frame cap 0.2. This ensures physics stability and real-time. Good.

But update also modifies `scenery` and elapsed in substeps; fine. Camera and spawn might push multiple same frame? okay. Audio maybe if multiple collisions. Fine.

However update called with dt .2 on state ready would then substep ready four times, okay. Effects fade. Fine.

Implement at top of `update`:
```
if (dt > 0.05) {
 const steps = Math.ceil(dt / 0.05);
 for (let i=0;i<steps;i++) update(dt/steps);
 return;
}
```
But if state paused and dt .2, effects update in substeps. fine. This ensures no tunneling at max 0.05 (23.5px). Great. Then cap frame at 0.25. If fps <4, time still slows, acceptable.

Let's add. Also in frame cap .25.

